  "body": "Part of #361 (vendor STEP tool models). First slice: milling.\n\n**Today:** a tool's holder is one cylinder (`Tool::holder_d` / `holder_len`, `crates/tools/src/tools.rs`). Every mill check (shank/holder reach, multi-axis avoidance, fixture sweeps) judges stacked cylinders. A real holder or extension modelled by its maker can't be used.\n\n**Proposed:**\n- **Import.** Attach a STEP (or STL) body to a tool as its holder/assembly through the existing importer (`app/geometry/import/step.rs`), as one undoable command.\n- **Placement.** The body is placed in the tool frame by numeric values: the axis it lies along, and the gauge/tip offset. No picking UI in this slice.\n- **Envelope.** Derive its revolved envelope: the largest radius of the body in each band along the tool axis, conservative by construction. The existing cylinder-stack checks then judge that profile instead of the single holder cylinder. A tool without a body behaves exactly as today.\n- **Persistence.** The body and its placement save and load in the project file (with a version rung if needed), and undo/redo walk it.\n- **Refusals by name:** degenerate or non-finite bodies, an empty mesh, and a body that doesn't reach the tool axis.\n\n**Done means:** a test holder STEP wider than its cylinder stand-in, at one height band, turns a move the cylinder passed into a named collision. A tool without a body is byte-identical to today. The project round-trips, undo restores the cylinder.\n\nNot in this slice: the tool library file (see #348), viewport display, lathe holders, ISO 13399 coordinate systems.\n",
